<div style="margin-bottom: var(--ui-space-4);" id="getting-started">
    <UiPanel Title="Getting started">
        <p>
            The dashboard doesn't start or stop containers itself — each machine runs its own
            <strong>supervisor</strong> (a small service alongside vLLM, started by your own compose file),
            and the dashboard talks to it over HTTP. Setting one up is three steps:
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <strong><a href="servers">Servers</a></strong> — register the machine (label, GPU info,
                models directory) and an engine instance on it (proxy slug, vLLM port, and the supervisor's own
                URL). Use <strong>[Test connection]</strong> before saving — it calls the supervisor
                directly and shows its GPU and current model, or a clear error if the URL is wrong or
                unreachable.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><a href="models-recipes">Models &amp; Recipes</a></strong> — add a model (a local
                path, or download one from Hugging Face through the supervisor) and create a recipe for it
                (context length, GPU memory utilization, dtype, and the rest of vLLM's own launch flags).
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><a href="">Overview</a></strong> — pick a recipe and swap it in. The swap ceremony
                shows a live diff, step-by-step progress as the supervisor reports it, and the result.
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted">
            A fresh install shows a checklist on the Overview page for exactly these steps — it disappears
            for good after your first swap.
        </p>
    </UiPanel>
</div>

<div style="margin-bottom: var(--ui-space-4);" id="how-it-works">
    <UiPanel Title="How it works">
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>No hot-swap, idle-unload, or preload settings</strong> — those were part of an
                earlier design where the dashboard managed containers directly. Now the supervisor owns the
                vLLM process's whole lifecycle; the dashboard only ever asks it to start, stop, or restart
                a given recipe, and reads back its state.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Restore last known good model on boot</strong> — a per-engine-instance toggle on the
                Servers page (once that engine instance's supervisor is registered). On, the supervisor
                automatically replays the last model that reached a healthy "ready" state when its
                container restarts. A run that failed or crashed is never replayed, and it gives up after 3
                attempts rather than retrying forever. Off, the engine instance just comes up idle and waits for
                you to swap something in.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>No token to manage</strong> — the supervisor's admin API binds to that machine's own
                loopback address, not the network, so there's nothing for the dashboard to authenticate with
                beyond reaching it at all. Run the dashboard and supervisor so they share that loopback (e.g.
                <code>network_mode: host</code>) rather than exposing the admin API on the LAN.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Changing an engine instance's proxy slug changes its URL</strong> — every client pointed at
                <code>/proxy/&lt;slug&gt;/v1</code> needs updating if you rename it. The Servers page warns
                you when you do this; it still saves the change.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </UiPanel>
</div>

