<div style="margin-bottom: var(--ui-space-4);" id="choosing-settings">
    <UiPanel Title="Choosing recipe settings">
        <p>
            Good settings depend on your GPU memory, the model, and how many people use it at once, so
            there is no single recipe that suits everyone. This is how to work them out for your own
            hardware: estimate, load, read what vLLM reports, then adjust.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h6">1. How much memory the weights need</h3>
        <p>
            Weights take roughly <strong>parameters × bytes per parameter</strong>: about 2 bytes for
            16-bit (<code>bfloat16</code>/<code>float16</code>), 1 for 8-bit (<code>fp8</code>/int8), and
            about 0.5 to 0.6 for 4-bit (AWQ, GPTQ, GGUF Q4). A 7B model is about 14&nbsp;GB in 16-bit and
            4 to 5&nbsp;GB in 4-bit. Multimodal models add a vision encoder on top. The size of the
            weights on disk is a good estimate; once loaded, vLLM prints the exact figure
            (<code>Model loading took … GiB memory</code>).
        </p>

        <h3 class="h6">2. GPU memory utilization: the budget</h3>
        <p>
            This is the fraction of the card's total memory vLLM may use for everything: weights,
            working memory for each step, CUDA graphs (if enabled), and the KV cache. What is left for
            the KV cache is:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@BudgetFormula" ShowCopy="false" />
        <p>
            Start at <strong>0.90</strong>. If the model barely fits, raise it toward <strong>0.93 to
            0.95</strong>; if other programs share the GPU, or vLLM fails at startup with an out-of-memory
            error, lower it. vLLM prints what it ended up with as
            <code>Available KV cache memory</code> and <code>GPU KV cache size: N tokens</code>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h6">3. Max model length: what the KV cache must hold</h3>
        <p>The KV cache stores two numbers (key and value) per layer for every token in every active conversation:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@KvFormula" ShowCopy="false" />
        <p>
            Layers, KV heads and head size are in the model's <code>config.json</code>
            (<code>num_hidden_layers</code>, <code>num_key_value_heads</code>, and
            <code>hidden_size ÷ num_attention_heads</code> unless <code>head_dim</code> is given). Models
            that use sliding-window or hybrid attention need less than this formula says.
            <strong>Max model length × the number of simultaneous requests</strong> should fit in that
            token count; vLLM prints <code>Maximum concurrency for N tokens per request: X×</code>, and
            you want X to be at least the number of people using it at once. If it is below 1, the
            model will not start at that length.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h6">A worked example (illustrative numbers, not a recommendation)</h3>
        <p>
            A 16&nbsp;GB card and an 8B model in 4-bit (about 5.5&nbsp;GB of weights) with 32 layers, 8 KV
            heads and head size 128, at utilization 0.90, with CUDA graphs on:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@WorkedExample" ShowCopy="false" />
        <p>
            So a max model length of 32768 fits once (about 1.6× concurrency) and 8192 fits about six
            times over. With Enforce eager on, the 1&nbsp;GB for graphs is freed and the cache grows by that much.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h6">4. The other settings</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Max batched tokens</strong> — how many tokens vLLM processes in one step. Larger
                values speed up long prompts but need more working memory. Typical values are 2048 to
                8192. Multimodal models need at least the per-image token count (see below).
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>max-num-seqs</code></strong> (an Extra arg) — how many conversations run at
                once. For one or two users, a small value (such as 2 to 4) reduces memory and the number of
                CUDA graphs captured. Raise it for many concurrent users.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>KV cache dtype</strong> — <code>fp8</code> halves the KV cache, so roughly twice the
                tokens fit, if your GPU and vLLM build support it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Data type</strong> — leave at <code>auto</code> unless the hint under the field
                (for example on the Intel stack) says otherwise.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Block size</strong> — leave at the default unless your hardware's vLLM build
                documents a different one (the Intel example in the README uses 64).
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Enforce eager</strong> — see <a href="#eager-vs-graphs">Eager mode vs CUDA graphs</a>
                below: speed against start-up time, memory, and the neuron map.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="h6">5. Load, read the log, adjust</h3>
        <p>Start conservative, swap, and read the start-up log (the swap dialog shows it live):</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>"the KV cache … is larger than the available KV cache memory … estimated maximum
                model length is N"</strong> — lower Max model length to about N, or raise the utilization,
                or use a smaller or more quantized model.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Out-of-memory while loading</strong> — lower the utilization, free GPU memory used by
                other programs, or use a smaller or more quantized model.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The load works but the KV cache is tiny</strong> (the concurrency figure is barely
                1×) — the same fixes, or turn Enforce eager on to free the graph memory.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="h6">Things that commonly bite</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Multimodal models need room for an image.</strong> vLLM refuses to start unless
                <em>Max batched tokens</em> is at least the model's per-image token count, which can be
                more than the default 2048 (one model needed 2496). The error reads
                <code>max_tokens_per_mm_item (…) &gt; max_num_batched_tokens</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Multimodal GGUF files may not load.</strong> vLLM's GGUF support is limited. If a
                model works in another runtime (such as Ollama) but not here, look for an AWQ or other
                safetensors build of it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Only use speculative decoding the model supports.</strong> A model without
                multi-token-prediction heads rejects the <code>mtp</code> method; leave speculative decoding
                off unless the model is meant for it.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted">
            The project README has a worked recipe for a large MoE model on an Intel GPU, and the specific
            models and settings that were measured on one NVIDIA test machine.
        </p>
    </UiPanel>
</div>

<div style="margin-bottom: var(--ui-space-4);" id="choosing-engine">
    <UiPanel Title="Choosing an engine (vLLM or llama.cpp)">
        <p>
            Each engine instance runs one engine, chosen on the <a href="servers">Servers</a> page. The last
            column says whether a row was checked against a real run or is general knowledge that nobody
            here has confirmed yet — treat <strong>Unverified</strong> rows as a starting point, not a fact.
        </p>
        <div class="ui-table-wrap" style="margin-bottom: var(--ui-space-3);">
            <table class="ui-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th scope="col">Topic</th><th scope="col">vLLM</th><th scope="col">llama.cpp</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>GGUF models</td>
                        <td>Limited. A multimodal Gemma 4 GGUF did not load in vLLM 0.33.</td>
                        <td>Native. A GGUF was served directly (Qwen2.5-0.5B, Q4_K_M).</td>
                        <td>Verified for llama.cpp; the vLLM result is from an earlier attempt</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Start-up time</td>
                        <td>Minutes on a slow CPU: imports, compile, warm-up.</td>
                        <td>Fast: a small model on CPU was listening about a second after launch. Large models were not timed.</td>
                        <td>Verified for a small model on CPU only</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>KV-cache and latency metrics</td>
                        <td>KV-cache usage, time to first token, inter-token and end-to-end latency, prefix-cache hits.</td>
                        <td>Its metrics have no KV-cache usage and no latency histograms, so the Overview shows KV cache as n/a.</td>
                        <td>Verified against a real server's metrics</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Start-up progress</td>
                        <td>Weight loading and CUDA-graph capture percentages, in four phases.</td>
                        <td>Three flat stages (starting, loading, ready) and no percentages, so the swap steps have no Warm up engine row.</td>
                        <td>Verified against a real start-up log</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Neuron map</td>
                        <td>Available (needs Enforce eager).</td>
                        <td>Not available — the plugin hooks vLLM.</td>
                        <td>Verified (by design)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Many simultaneous users</td>
                        <td>Strong: continuous batching, efficient KV cache.</td>
                        <td>A fixed number of parallel slots that share the context (4 in the test run).</td>
                        <td>Unverified — the slot count was seen, scaling was not measured</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Models larger than VRAM</td>
                        <td>Mostly no.</td>
                        <td>Yes: a GPU and RAM split, slower.</td>
                        <td>Unverified — not tried</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>GPU memory</td>
                        <td>Reserves a large fixed fraction up front.</td>
                        <td>Uses what it needs.</td>
                        <td>Unverified — the test run was CPU only</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Hardware support</td>
                        <td>Needs a supported GPU stack.</td>
                        <td>CPU, Vulkan, Intel, NVIDIA and more.</td>
                        <td>Unverified — only CPU was run</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Features</td>
                        <td>Tool and reasoning parsers, speculative decoding, prefix caching, broad multimodal.</td>
                        <td>A different set, still growing.</td>
                        <td>Unverified — not compared</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="mb-0">
            <strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> many simultaneous users on a model that fits — use vLLM. One or
            two users, a big GGUF, or a model bigger than VRAM — use llama.cpp.
        </p>
    </UiPanel>
</div>

<div style="margin-bottom: var(--ui-space-4);" id="eager-vs-graphs">
    <UiPanel Title="Eager mode vs CUDA graphs (and the neuron map)">
        <p class="text-muted">vLLM only — llama.cpp has no CUDA-graph or eager setting and no neuron map.</p>
        <ul class="mb-0">
            <li>
                <strong>Enforce eager on</strong> runs the model one operation at a time from Python. It
                loads quickly and is the safest setting, but on a machine with a slow CPU it can be the
                bottleneck: vLLM waits on one busy CPU core while the GPU sits partly idle.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Enforce eager off</strong> lets vLLM compile the model and capture CUDA graphs,
                which replays the whole forward pass in one go. It was much faster for generation on the
                same box, at the cost of a longer first start (the swap's <em>Warm up engine</em> step shows
                the compile and graph capture) and more GPU memory (graphs come out of the utilization budget, see step 2 above).
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The Overview neuron map only works with Enforce eager on.</strong> It reads each
                layer through Python hooks, and those do not run inside a captured CUDA graph or compiled
                code, so the map stays blank. Set <code>VIS_ENABLED</code> = <code>0</code> in the recipe's
                environment vars for a graph recipe so the plugin stays out of the way, and keep a separate
                eager recipe for the model when you want to watch the map.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </UiPanel>
</div>

<div id="troubleshooting">
    <UiPanel Title="Troubleshooting">
        <ul class="mb-0">
            <li>
                <strong>"Test connection" fails</strong> — confirm the supervisor URL is reachable from this
                dashboard (not just from your own machine). Since the admin API only listens on loopback,
                this almost always means the dashboard and supervisor containers aren't sharing a network
                namespace (e.g. one isn't on <code>network_mode: host</code>) rather than a firewall issue.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>An engine instance shows "needs supervisor registration"</strong> — it exists in the
                dashboard (often from an earlier version's data) but has no supervisor URL saved yet.
                Its proxy URL keeps forwarding traffic either way; only swapping is blocked until you
                register it on the Servers page.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A model loads but uses far more VRAM than expected</strong> — check whether GPU
                memory utilization and max model length are set explicitly on the recipe. Left at their
                defaults, vLLM may fall back to the model's own (sometimes very large) native context length.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The neuron map is blank but the model answers</strong> — the recipe almost certainly
                has <em>Enforce eager</em> off (CUDA graphs); the map needs eager mode. See
                <a href="#eager-vs-graphs">Eager mode vs CUDA graphs</a>. Also check that the plugin isn't
                switched off (<code>VIS_ENABLED=0</code>, or <strong>Plugin on/off</strong> in the Neuron map
                section).
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Loading weights takes minutes</strong> — vLLM reads the whole model from disk.
                The first load of a model that isn't in the operating system's file cache is limited by
                your disk (about 55&nbsp;MB/s from a busy spinning disk: a 7.7&nbsp;GB model took 2.5 minutes
                where a cached load took 3 seconds). Loading it again soon after is much faster. The swap
                steps show real progress: <em>Start new process</em> (vLLM importing, about a minute on a
                slow CPU), <em>Load weights</em>, <em>Warm up engine</em>, then <em>Health check</em>. A llama.cpp
                engine instance has no <em>Warm up engine</em> step and shows no percentages while loading.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A swap seems stuck</strong> — the first load of an uncached Hugging Face model
                downloads its weights first, which can take a long time on a slow connection; the swap
                ceremony's progress steps and live log view show exactly where it is. If it's genuinely
                stuck, Cancel is always available and leaves the engine instance in a known state (serving the old
                model, or idle, depending on how far the swap got).
            </li>
        </ul>
    </UiPanel>
</div>

@code {
    private const string BudgetFormula = """
KV cache memory = total memory × utilization
                  − weights
                  − working memory      (roughly 1 to 2 GB, more for multimodal or large batches)
                  − CUDA graphs         (roughly 0.5 to 2 GB; none when Enforce eager is on)
""";

    private const string KvFormula = """
KV bytes per token = 2 × layers × KV heads × head size × bytes per value
                     (16-bit values = 2 bytes; fp8 KV cache = 1 byte)
tokens that fit    = KV cache memory ÷ KV bytes per token
""";

    private const string WorkedExample = """
budget          = 16 × 0.90                  = 14.4 GB
KV cache        = 14.4 − 5.5 − 1.5 − 1.0     =  6.4 GB
KV per token    = 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 bytes = 128 KB
tokens that fit = 6.4 GB ÷ 128 KB            ≈ 52,000 tokens
""";
}
